Deliver CSP as a response header and begin with a policy that limits resource types, commonly using `default-src 'self'`, then add only required sources. For scripts, prefer a nonce- or hash-based strict policy tied to the exact scripts allowed for that response; broad host allowlists are easier to bypass or misconfigure. Add directives such as `connect-src`, `img-src`, and `frame-ancestors` according to the application, and test reporting before enforcement where appropriate. CSP reduces XSS impact but does not replace contextual output encoding or HTML sanitization. Apply it consistently to responses, not only the main document.

```ts caption="Illustrative server-side nonce policy; the nonce must be unpredictable and match only scripts intentionally emitted in that response."
const nonce = crypto.randomUUID().replaceAll("-", "");
res.setHeader("Content-Security-Policy", `default-src 'self'; script-src 'nonce-${nonce}'`);
return `<script nonce="${nonce}">${trustedBootstrap}</script>`;
```
